WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.338
I brought the Ellen Orrin in to the Tamarisk steps at ten minutes to midnight,

00:00:04.338 --> 00:00:05.840
and I made her fast myself.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.040
Two turns round the bollard.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:09.260
A hitch.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.320
Then a third turn, for luck.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.595
My brother Daniel showed me that knot, though he was the younger by four years,

00:00:16.595 --> 00:00:18.480
and he never once let me forget it.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.240
I had tied it every night for thirty-one years.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.380
It seemed a poor night to stop.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.320
It was the night of the county election.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.039
The polls had closed at nine, and every other boat on the Orrin had gone in by ten,

00:00:32.039 --> 00:00:35.280
so the crews could get to the bars and the wireless sets.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.380
I was the last ferry.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.000
The company kept me on out of habit.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.560
I kept myself on for the same reason.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.680
I waited on the wet stones and listened.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.891
The Tamarisk Building is seven floors of soot-black brick on the north bank,

00:00:50.891 --> 00:00:53.360
and the Meridian Courier takes the top three.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.560
The presses are in the cellar, below the tide line.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.339
They run every night of the year, and you feel them through your boots before you

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:01.939
hear them.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.340
A slow iron turning.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.740
A sound with no hurry in it.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.120
I went in by the side door.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.893
The porter's box in the lobby was empty,

00:01:11.893 --> 00:01:15.280
though his lamp was lit and his evening paper lay open on the stool.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.280
A card hung on the lift gate.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.540
Out of Order, it said, in brown ink gone pale with age.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:24.360
I took the stairs.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.400
On the third landing a boy ran down past me with a bundle of copy under his arm.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:30.940
He did not look at me.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.740
On the fourth I stopped to get my breath.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.065
On the fifth a girl in a canteen apron was wheeling a tea urn towards the service

00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:41.940
lift, and she said good evening, and called me sir.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.780
I am fifty-eight years old.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.365
Daniel was fifty-four when they took him out of the river three weeks ago,

00:01:49.365 --> 00:01:52.600
and I had still not grown used to being the one who was left.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.580
The newsroom ran the length of the seventh floor.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:59.237
Green-shaded lamps hung low over the desks,

00:01:59.237 --> 00:02:02.060
and tobacco smoke lay flat under the ceiling.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.680
A clock on the end wall said ten past twelve.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.840
I took out my father's watch to check it, and it agreed.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:14.420
Nobody looked up as I went down the room, and I was glad of it.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.140
I had come for one strip of paper.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.400
I meant to be back on my boat inside ten minutes.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.700
The night editor's desk was at the far end, under the clock.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.420
A woman sat behind it, narrow and upright,

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.140
in a grey cardigan buttoned to the throat.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.280
A brass plate on the blotter said ALMA PENHALLOW, NIGHT EDITOR.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.465
She was marking a galley with a blue pencil,

00:02:39.465 --> 00:02:42.500
and the pencil did not pause when I stopped in front of her.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.500
"My name's Leonard Kendrick," I said.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.520
"I've come for a death notice.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.880
My brother, Daniel Kendrick.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.420
Your front counter told me on Tuesday the proof would be ready tonight."

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.180
"It's ready," said Alma.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.980
She slid a long strip of proof across the blotter without raising her head.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.140
It stopped against my knuckles.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.263
I looked down and saw his name in black capitals, and his dates,

00:03:08.263 --> 00:03:11.140
and under them a line I had never written.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:13.800
"I didn't give you that," I said.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.180
"That last line.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.400
That isn't mine."

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.340
"No, Mr Kendrick.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:19.980
Her pencil went on.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:21.820
"The river set that one."

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.180
I waited for her to look up.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:29.136
When at last she did, I found I had been waiting for something smaller than her

00:03:29.136 --> 00:03:29.400
face.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.800
A little ordinary movement.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.880
The kind you only notice when it fails to come.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.740
She had not blinked since I walked in.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.620
She did not blink now.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.259
I stayed where I was, at Alma's desk under the clock,

00:03:45.259 --> 00:03:48.020
with my brother's proof beneath my hand.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.540
I should have folded it and gone.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:57.470
Instead I read it again, slowly, because it was Daniel's name and I had paid four

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:58.740
shillings for it.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.080
DANIEL KENDRICK, FERRYMAN.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.560
Beloved son and brother.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.260
Then his dates.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.600
Then the line I had not written.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:10.980
He crossed on a promise.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:14.240
"Sit down if you like," said Alma.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:16.420
She had gone back to her galley.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:18.200
"There's more of it."

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:19.540
"More?

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.420
I paid for three lines."

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:23.400
"The proof runs long.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:24.640
Turn it over."

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:26.280
I turned it.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.267
On the back, in a smaller face, the print went on in close columns,

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:33.980
and I held it nearer the desk lamp to read.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.040
The lamp burned without the least flicker.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.401
I noticed that and put it aside, the way you put aside a stone in your shoe until

00:04:42.401 --> 00:04:43.620
you have time for it.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.420
Then I laid the proof down on her blotter.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:48.480
I wanted both hands free.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.580
I could not have told you why.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.300
That was when I noticed the room.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.480
There were eleven of them at the long desks.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.320
Seven men in shirtsleeves and braces.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.800
Two women with their hair pinned up.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.760
A clerk with a pencil behind his ear, and a boy at the end.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.020
Every one of them was typing.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.740
I had heard it as I came in and taken it for rain on the skylights.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.540
It was not rain.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.533
It was eleven machines striking together, key for key,

00:05:18.533 --> 00:05:21.620
and the carriages coming back on the same instant.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.560
Eleven bells, rung as one.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:28.420
Then I looked at their faces, and that was worse.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.380
Their lips were moving.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.420
They were not muttering, and they were not chewing.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:35.480
They were shaping words.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:41.360
Each mouth formed a word as each key came down, and not one of them made a sound.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:45.741
A heavy man by the window rounded his lips on something long,

00:05:45.741 --> 00:05:48.180
and his hands struck it out a moment after.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.240
"It's the draught," I said aloud.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.380
I did not know who I was telling.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.580
"There's a draught under that door.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.780
It gets at the lamps, and the light plays on their faces."

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.840
No paper lifted on any desk.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:07.300
The window beside the heavy man was shut and painted over, and had been for years.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.460
"Then it's tiredness," I said.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.360
"Men on a long shift fall into a rhythm.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:14.640
Rowers do."

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.040
"Yes," said Alma, without looking up.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:19.280
"They do."

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:24.670
Against the far wall was a teletype in a glass case, and as I watched,

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:25.780
it began to tick.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:27.900
I went over to it.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.300
I wanted something to do with my feet.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:34.149
The paper came up out of the machine in a pale tongue,

00:06:34.149 --> 00:06:36.880
and I tore off a length and held it to the light.

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.660
River Orrin. 4.10 a.m.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:42.360
Flat calm.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:43.940
A crossing made.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.040
I looked at my watch.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:47.500
Ten past twelve.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.700
It was hours yet to four.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:52.800
And the machine had no wire clerk to tend it.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.487
Its cable hung loose from the back of the case and lay coiled on the boards,

00:06:57.487 --> 00:06:58.600
plugged into nothing.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:01.400
"It's a custom," I said.

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:03.200
My voice had thinned.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:05.260
"Some night-shift joke.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:06.740
For election night."

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:08.820
Nobody answered me.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:10.700
The teletype ticked on.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.360
I tore off a second length, and a third, and the third had my family on it.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.460
KENDRICK, MARGIT.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:23.140
My mother, drowned off the coal staithes in the winter of 'twenty-nine.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.580
KENDRICK, ILSE, aged seven.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:28.740
KENDRICK, JOSEPH.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.540
My father, who stopped speaking the year before he died.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:34.840
KENDRICK, DANIEL.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:40.440
They ran down the paper in a column, one to a line, in the order they had gone.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.420
After each name came the same few words, in the same plain type.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:47.060
Voice owed.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:48.500
Not yet paid.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:50.300
I counted them.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:51.560
Four lines.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:53.820
Then a fifth, still printing.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.820
The letters pressed up out of the machine one at a time while I watched them come.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:00.800
It was my name.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:06.580
I went back to Alma's desk with the teletype paper crushed in my fist.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.000
The clock above her still said ten past twelve.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:14.620
I looked at my watch again, and it said ten past twelve as well.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:18.140
It had said so, I think, for some while.

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:21.440
"You'll come down with me," I told her.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:22.500
"Now.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:25.880
We'll fetch the porter, and then the police."

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:28.480
Alma laid her blue pencil across the galley.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:30.200
She had stopped marking.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.500
"Sit down, Mr Kendrick," she said.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.380
"You've waited long enough for the plain version."

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:38.220
I didn't sit.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:39.960
She told me anyway.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:43.300
"Your brother was nineteen," said Alma.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.680
"It was January, and the Orrin was running ice.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:50.796
Your father was ill, so Daniel took the ferry over alone,

00:08:50.796 --> 00:08:54.140
with a doctor's bag for a woman in labour on the south bank.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:58.180
Halfway across, the floes closed round the boat.

00:08:58.900 --> 00:08:59.940
He couldn't make headway.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:01.860
So he spoke to the river.

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:04.000
Men do, out there."

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:06.180
"What did he say to it?"

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:10.480
"He said, get me over, and you can have my voice.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:12.680
She folded her hands.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:14.740
"He meant he'd pray.

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:18.160
He meant he'd sing hymns at the tiller, or some such.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.540
He thought it was a manner of speaking.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:22.740
The river took it as a bargain."

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.740
Behind her the typing went on, eleven keys to the beat.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:30.280
"He crossed," said Alma.

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:32.560
"The ice opened in front of him.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:35.260
He lived another thirty-five years.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:40.520
Then three weeks ago he didn't, and the river came looking for what it was owed."

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.080
"He's dead," I said.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:45.380
"Whatever he owed, it's settled."

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:49.300
"A man can't pay with a voice he no longer has.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:53.300
She said it kindly, and that was the worst of it.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:56.960
"It doesn't want coin, and it doesn't want blood.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:09:59.080
It wants its words said.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:02.901
The river dictates, and someone has to take it down,

00:10:02.901 --> 00:10:06.160
every night until the dawn edition goes to the presses.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:07.800
Then the next night.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.000
Then the next."

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:10.920
"For how long?"

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:12.360
"For good."

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:14.820
I looked along the desks.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:20.020
A bald man near the window had a wedding ring worn thin as wire on his typing hand.

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.300
He had not raised his eyes once since I came in.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:24.960
"And these?

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:25.760
I said.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:26.820
"Who are they?"

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.260
"The ones who were nearest when it asked.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.580
Alma pressed the proof flat on her blotter.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:39.240
"It asked your mother, and your sister, and your father, each in turn.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:41.640
None of them would stay to answer.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:46.000
So it kept on asking, and took whoever was close by."

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:49.140
Something touched the toe of my boot.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:50.700
I looked down.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:55.100
A dark thread of river was crossing the boards from the landing door.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:59.140
It ran in a straight line, in no hurry at all.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:04.280
It reached the leg of the nearest desk, divided, and went round it.

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:06.580
The landing door was open.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:10.000
I crossed to it and looked down the stairwell.

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:13.940
Six steps below me, the stairs went under.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:17.430
Black flood lay there, flat and still,

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:20.760
with the smell of the mud flats rising off it.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:24.220
While I watched, it took the fifth step.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:25.860
Then the fourth.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:28.160
The presses had stopped.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:29.860
I hadn't noticed when.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:33.776
There was nothing under the floor now at all,

00:11:33.776 --> 00:11:37.060
and the silence made the keys behind me very loud.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.380
"That's the only way out," I said.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:42.700
"It is," said Alma.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:44.520
"It always has been."

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:50.480
The flood rose another step, and behind me the teletype began to tick.

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:55.800
I came back to the night editor's desk without meaning to.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:59.200
The flood had reached the third step from the landing.

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:05.160
Alma sat as she had sat all night, and the proof lay flat under her hand.

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:08.760
"There must be a way to pay it," I said.

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:10.920
"You said yourself it was owed."

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:13.340
"I said it was owed.

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:15.400
I never said you could pay it.

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:19.253
She turned her head towards the window, slowly,

00:12:19.253 --> 00:12:21.720
as if her neck had set in one position.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:25.200
"I'm the last it asked, Mr Kendrick.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.320
I sit in this chair.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:31.760
I haven't left it, and I haven't tried to, for a very long time.

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:35.660
The river wanted a pair of hands to take its words down.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:37.240
It has had mine.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:40.040
And still it isn't satisfied."

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:41.780
"Why not?"

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:44.760
"Because I'm nearly worn through.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:48.760
She lifted her right hand off the proof and showed me.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:53.520
The tips of her fingers were worn smooth, with no prints left on them.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:55.680
"Hands wear out."

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:01.440
Behind us the teletype ticked, and at the end of a line a single bell rang.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:03.560
I did not answer her.

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:07.860
I had begun to look about the room the way a drowning man looks for a rope.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:11.180
There was the door, and the flood behind it.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:15.660
There were eleven desks and eleven mouths moving on one beat.

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:20.533
There was the lamp on Alma's desk, a brass thing on a jointed arm,

00:13:20.533 --> 00:13:23.680
bent low over her typewriter and lighting nothing.

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:25.350
Then a cup rattled.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:30.570
The girl from the fifth landing had come in at the service door with a tin tray in

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:31.270
both hands.

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:33.530
She was perhaps seventeen.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:38.570
Her name was stitched on her apron in red thread: KATHLEEN VENABLES.

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:42.910
She had brought tea, God help her, eleven cups of it,

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:47.830
and the cups were chattering in their saucers because her arms had begun to shake.

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:51.030
Her face had gone the colour of proof paper.

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:53.330
She told me the stairs were wet.

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:56.222
She told me she couldn't hear the presses any more,

00:13:56.222 --> 00:13:57.990
and asked me what had happened to them.

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:00.470
"Stay where you are," I said.

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:01.710
"Don't come any closer."

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:03.730
She came closer.

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:05.170
People do.

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:09.730
Alma's eyes moved once to the girl, then back to me.

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:12.610
I will not pretend she urged me on.

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:14.610
She said nothing at all.

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:19.686
It was the silence of a woman who had watched this choice made before,

00:14:19.686 --> 00:14:22.530
and knew the shape of it, and was only waiting.

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:25.790
I thought of Ilse, who was seven.

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:30.110
I thought of my father at the tiller, the winter he stopped speaking.

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:34.650
I thought of the flood on the stairs, and how quickly it was climbing.

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:39.410
I crossed the floor in five strides and took Kathleen by the shoulders.

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:41.210
The tray went down.

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:44.070
China and hot tea went across the boards.

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:48.693
I turned her round, walked her backwards to the night editor's desk,

00:14:48.693 --> 00:14:50.450
and sat her down in the chair.

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:53.350
Alma was simply no longer in it.

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:55.270
I never saw her rise.

00:14:56.230 --> 00:14:57.630
The chair received the girl.

00:14:58.330 --> 00:14:59.690
There is no other word for it.

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:03.410
Her back met the slats, and her spine straightened.

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:08.268
Her hands came up out of her lap as if someone had lifted them by wires,

00:15:08.268 --> 00:15:09.990
and they came down on the keys.

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:13.590
The typewriter woke with one long rattle.

00:15:14.570 --> 00:15:15.570
Her lips parted.

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:19.730
They made one word, and I knew it, though no sound came.

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:21.190
Please.

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:24.630
Then they began to make the river's words instead.

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:27.490
Above her the brass lamp stirred.

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:32.763
It swung down on its jointed arm, slowly, with a small dry creak,

00:15:32.763 --> 00:15:36.230
until its hot rim lay across the backs of her hands.

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:38.390
It pressed them to the keys.

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:40.050
It held them there.

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:44.030
Along the room, the eleven mouths changed their beat.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:45.990
They had found a twelfth.

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:50.050
Alma was by the painted window with her back to all of us.

00:15:50.930 --> 00:15:53.010
At last, she closed her eyes.

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:58.450
Before I went, I bent and drew Daniel's proof out from under the girl's wrist.

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:00.830
It slid free without resistance.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:02.650
She did not stir.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:07.430
I folded it once, and again, and put it inside my coat.

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:11.270
I did not look at Kathleen again.

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:13.510
I had learned that much already.

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:16.590
You do not look at what you have paid with.

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:22.655
I went out onto the landing with the proof against my ribs and put my hand on the

00:16:22.655 --> 00:16:23.255
rail.

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:26.870
The stairs ran down into the dark, and they were dry.

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:31.650
I tried each step with my boot before I trusted my weight to it.

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:36.081
At the fourth landing I stopped and listened for the flood,

00:16:36.081 --> 00:16:38.010
because I had watched it climb.

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:39.750
There was none.

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:43.243
Below me the presses had started again,

00:16:43.243 --> 00:16:47.270
and their long iron beat came up through the soles of my boots.

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:50.690
In the lobby I turned and looked back.

00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:56.070
The bottom stairs were wet, the way stone is wet after a mop has been over it.

00:16:56.690 --> 00:17:00.430
A dark tidemark ran along the skirting at the height of my knee.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:01.830
That was all.

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:04.390
Nothing had climbed those stairs tonight.

00:17:05.110 --> 00:17:06.510
Nothing had needed to.

00:17:07.430 --> 00:17:08.030
"Hello?

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:10.310
I called towards the porter's box.

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:12.010
"Is anybody about?"

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:14.410
His lamp burned on.

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:19.320
His paper still lay open on the stool, the type black and clean,

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:21.470
and I did not let myself read it.

00:17:22.090 --> 00:17:26.110
I pushed the street door and went out onto the quay.

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:27.010
It was not yet light.

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:30.752
The sky over the Orrin was the colour of old pewter,

00:17:30.752 --> 00:17:33.890
and the lamps along the embankment were still burning.

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:38.424
The Ellen Orrin lay where I had left her, bow to the steps,

00:17:38.424 --> 00:17:40.690
two turns and a hitch on the bollard.

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:44.610
I stepped aboard, and she took my weight without a sound.

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:47.530
I cast off the way Daniel had shown me.

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:50.230
The loop first, then the long pull in.

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:51.870
My hands were steady.

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:54.790
I noticed that, and I did not like it.

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:58.530
I started the engine and took her out into the stream.

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:03.970
I have crossed that river some thousands of times, and I know every voice it has.

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:06.210
It slaps under the piers.

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:08.950
It sucks at the turn by the old cement works.

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:14.624
On a windy night it talks along the hull in a low grumble that never quite makes

00:18:14.624 --> 00:18:15.224
words.

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:18.310
That morning it did none of these things.

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:21.030
It lay flat from bank to bank.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:26.650
My wake spread behind me in two clean lines, and no sound came off it.

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:29.330
I leaned out and put my hand in.

00:18:30.050 --> 00:18:34.010
The river let me in, and let me go, and kept its mouth shut.

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:38.390
The sun came up as I reached mid-channel.

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:43.330
It laid a long band of copper down the river, and I steered along it.

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:46.530
The debt is gone, I told myself.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:49.190
I waited to feel it leave me.

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:51.710
Nothing in me moved at all.

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:56.270
I made the south bank at a quarter past six and shut off the engine.

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:59.190
The quiet came down over the boat.

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:03.150
"It's done," I said aloud, to nobody.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:04.810
"Do you hear me?

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:06.070
It's done."

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:09.290
The river took the words without an echo.

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:12.130
I turned and looked back across.

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:15.410
From there the Tamarisk Building was small.

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:19.482
Its windows had caught the sunrise and gone to gold,

00:19:19.482 --> 00:19:22.710
every one but a single window on the seventh floor.

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:28.430
That one was lit from inside, a steady yellow, and I knew the lamp.

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:30.690
Someone sat beneath it.

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:34.610
I could make out the shape of her head, bent forward a little.

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:36.550
I could see her hands.

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:37.790
They were moving.

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:39.630
And I could hear them.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:43.702
A quarter of a mile of flat river lay between us,

00:19:43.702 --> 00:19:47.410
and I heard her keys as plainly as if she sat at my elbow.

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:50.990
Eleven machines, and a twelfth.

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:52.970
My jaw ached.

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:57.110
I put my palm against it and felt it working under the skin.

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:00.990
My lips were shaping every word her keys struck.

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:02.550
No.

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:04.110
They were shaping it first.

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:08.030
I knew each word a breath before she set it down.

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:10.410
"Stop," I said.

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:12.290
The word came out.

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:17.130
Then my mouth went on without me, quietly, in its own order.

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.350
I do not know how long I had been doing it.

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:22.050
Since the stairs, perhaps.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:23.630
Since the chair.

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:27.290
There had been no sound, and so I had not heard it.

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:29.610
I clamped my teeth shut.

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:32.330
My mouth went on moving behind them.

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:36.831
Daniel worked the tiller for thirty-five years with his lips going,

00:20:36.831 --> 00:20:38.830
and I always thought he was praying.

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:41.130
Alma was never the voice.

00:20:41.650 --> 00:20:42.970
She was only the hands.

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:46.903
There had always been a mouth as well, out on the river,

00:20:46.903 --> 00:20:50.910
and it had been empty since the night they took my brother out of the reeds.

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:54.210
It was not empty any more.

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:59.330
I took the proof out of my coat, there on the south bank, with the sun full on it.

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:02.210
I wanted to see the girl's name in print.

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:05.810
A man goes to look at a gravestone for the same reason.

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:08.550
Daniel's notice was at the head of the strip.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:11.230
Kathleen's was set beneath it.

00:21:12.150 --> 00:21:15.670
Below hers was a third, and the ink came off on my thumb.

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:17.750
It had no name.

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:23.510
There was only a space, the width of a short name, left open for the compositor.

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:27.070
Under the space, the line was already set.

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:29.950
Heard it read aloud, and kept still.

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:32.817
That was what the river had lacked,

00:21:32.817 --> 00:21:36.790
in all the years Daniel mouthed at it with nobody near enough to hear.

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:38.490
It had hands.

00:21:39.110 --> 00:21:39.830
It had a mouth.

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:42.110
It had never had an ear.

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:44.830
Her hands have not stopped since.

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:46.510
Nor has my mouth.

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:50.106
I have said every word of this in her order,

00:21:50.106 --> 00:21:54.330
and the voice you are hearing now has not been mine for some time.

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:56.990
She is setting down the last of it.

00:21:57.890 --> 00:21:59.310
You have kept very still.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:02.990
I have watched the space on the proof fill while you listened.

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:06.710
Letter by letter, in the same plain type.

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:08.390
It's your name.

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:09.690
It fits.

